theory:
## Postconditions with ensures

A postcondition must hold AFTER the function executes.

### Basic Syntax
```xiom
fn absolute(x: Int) -> Int
  ensures: result >= 0;
{
  if x < 0 { return -x; }
  return x;
}
```

`result` is a special keyword inside ensures clauses — it refers to the return value.

### Multiple ensures Clauses
```xiom
fn absolute(x: Int) -> Int
  ensures: result >= 0;
  ensures: result == x || result == -x;
{ if x < 0 { return -x; } return x; }
```

### ensures on Mutable Parameters
```xiom
fn increment(counter: &mut Int)
  ensures: counter == counter@pre + 1;
{ counter = counter + 1; }
```

`counter@pre` refers to the parameter's value BEFORE the function executed.

### ensures for Void Functions
Even void functions can have ensures clauses — for example, verifying a log was written or a file was closed.

syntax_ref:
fn name(params) -> ReturnType
  ensures: condition_about_result;
  ensures: condition_about_params@pre;
{ return value; }

